-- `xs[i]` as a class - Core's `Index(c -> k, v)`, Implementation-Containers.md §17.
--
-- Subscript used to be three hard-coded names dispatched through the ordinary overload set, which
-- worked for every concrete container and for no generic one: inside `fn f(xs: c)` there is no
-- overload set to key on, because `c` is a variable. The class answers that, and the functional
-- dependency is what makes it selectable at all - nothing at `xs[i]` says what a key or an element
-- is, so `c` has to decide both.

import Native

data Cell = Cell {value: Int, tag: Int}

-- The owner and the borrow of one both index, through two instances rather than a conversion:
-- selecting an instance does not widen its arguments, so an owner that is to be a `c` has to be one.
fn fromOwner(xs: Array(Int)) -> Int = xs[0]

fn fromSlice(xs: [Int]) -> Int = xs[0]

-- A fixed array has no instance of its own - `[a *n]` is structural, so it cannot be an instance
-- head - and reaches the class through its slice. That is the one conversion left.
fn fromFixed(xs: [Int *3]) -> Int = xs[1]

{-
   An element that is an aggregate, read through the borrow `get` handed back.

   The field is a projection off that borrow rather than off a copy of the element, which is the
   same place the *write* below names. Reading one used to ask for storage a returned borrow never
   has and report that the value had no address.
-}
fn fieldOfElement(xs: [Cell]) -> Int = xs[0].value + xs[1].tag

fn writeElement(&xs: [Cell]) -> {}:
    xs[0] = Cell {value: 7, tag: 8}
    xs[1].value = 9

{-
   The case the class exists for: a subscript inside a generic body.

   `c` is this function's own variable, so nothing here can name an overload set. What answers is
   the constraint the signature declared - and `k` and `v` are read off it rather than inferred,
   which is what the arrow in `class Index(c -> k, v)` promises.
-}
fn (Index(c, k, v), Num(v)) sumOfTwo(xs: c, a: k, b: k) -> v = xs[a] + xs[b]

-- And a caller of one. The literal keys take the container's key type rather than their own
-- default: `c` decides `k`, so the dependency answers before the literal settles.
fn viaConstraint(xs: Array(Int)) -> Int = sumOfTwo(xs, 0, 1)

-- A raw pointer indexes too, which the old type test rejected outright: `only an array may be
-- subscripted` was a rule about arrays, and this is a rule about instances.
{-
   The write goes through `getMut`, and `&p` is what roots it: a pointer has no owner the checker
   knows about, so what a mutable subscript borrows is the variable holding the address. Nothing
   about that is checked against the storage - which is the point, and why this lives in `Native`.

   The slice is what keeps it honest here. `values(xs)` is an address into whatever the caller's
   array was, and the parameter's own loan covers the call - so the run outlives every use of `p`
   for a reason the checker can state, rather than because the source happened to be written in the
   right order.
-}
fn throughPointer(&xs: [Int]) -> Int:
    let &p = values(xs)
    p[2] = 5
    return p[2]

fn main() -> Int:
    let &xs = [10, 20, 30]
    xs[2] = 40

    let &cells = [Cell {value: 1, tag: 2}, Cell {value: 3, tag: 4}]
    writeElement(cells)

    let &store = [0, 0, 0]

    -- 10 + 40 + (10 + 20) + (7 + 4) + 5 + 2 + 10 = 108.
    return fromOwner(xs) + xs[2] + sumOfTwo(xs, 0, 1) + fieldOfElement(cells) +
           throughPointer(store) + fromFixed([1, 2, 3] :: [Int *3]) + fromSlice(xs)
